{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Two things this turn. First, the per-mode execution (CheckRun 420bbfc415a14e20ad9a6689d1775917, exit 0) gave a complete comparison chain for the runtime question — control `holds`, mode B `holds`, mode C `violated` with valid witness index 9 and a complete witness — so I appended the current account on that basis: `append_notify` can resolve `Ok(assigned_idx)` for an entry that exists in no decided log, the same lost-entry state instead yields the documented `Superseded` when a tick falls between the origin's promise change and its decided-index advance, and the repository's own async example (which announces the returned index as 'durably decided ... safe to announce') relies on exactly that signal. Second, I opened an independent core-crate lead: `LeaderState` sizes `accepted_indexes` by the largest node id and `get_min_all_accepted_idx` minimises over that whole vector, so with a configuration such as {1,2,5} the unused slots for ids 3 and 4 pin the trim index at 0 — `trim(None)` reports success while compacting nothing and the documented `trim(Some(idx))` repair returns `NotAllDecided(0)`. I built and locally validated a two-case probe (control {1,2,3} advances compaction; treatment {1,2,5} does not, 0.00s runtime) and submitted it as the fixed experiment. Incomplete: that trim receipt is pending, and the tick-period/round-trip bound on the runtime defect's `ok` arm remains unmeasured beyond 2 ms and 80 ms.\"}"}}
